<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oshun</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ọṣun, Ochún, Oxúm</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oshun is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> of love, fertility, water, divination, wealth and beauty in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> religion, and the patron of the Osun River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, which bears her name; she is one of the most popular and venerated of the orishas. According to traditional beliefs she was once a queen of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shango">Shango</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> and was deified after her death. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifa">Ifá</a> literary corpus she is the only female primordial spirit sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olorun">Olodumare</a> to help create the world. She is honoured each August at the two-week <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osun-osogbo">Osun-Osogbo</a> Festival in the sacred grove at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Osogbo</a>, and her worship continues in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oshun.jpg" alt="Oshun" width="330" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikipedia Loves Art participant &quot; Department_of_Trife · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WLA_haa_Kneeling_Female_Figure_Devotee_of_Oshun_Yoruba_people_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to the Ifá Literary Corpus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olorun">Olodumare</a>, the Supreme God, sent a company of Irunmole, primordial spirits, to create the world, and Oshun was the only woman among them, sent to assist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shango">Shango</a>. The other spirits ignored her, and she went to Shango for guidance. Accounts of what followed differ. In one version the female spirits were tempted to act on their own, and all their creative attempts failed because they acted without male spiritual leadership.</p><p>In another version, more consistent with the start of the story, the male spirits tried to make the world without female influence, and it was this exclusion that made the world fail. In both tellings the myth ends with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shango">Shango</a> forcing the other spirits to respect Oshun as they would respect him. After her death, believers hold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olorun">Olodumare</a> granted her the powers of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Oshun is counted among the 401 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orishas</a>, and her devotees also associate her with sexuality, femininity, destiny and purity. Her river, the Osun, has its source in Ekiti State in the west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and passes through the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osogbo">Osogbo</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osun-osogbo">Osun-Osogbo Sacred Grove</a>, her principal sanctuary, stands on its banks. In the Regla de Ocha tradition a type of musical ceremony called a violín is performed for her, joining European classical music with Cuban popular music.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the Afro-Brazilian religions Oshun kept her role as goddess of love, beauty and waters and gained power over wealth and prosperity. Followers bring her romantic problems, and she is held responsible for marriage and other relationships. As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> of financial life she is called the Lady of Gold, a title that once referred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> as the most valuable metal of the time. She is worshipped at rivers and waterfalls, more rarely near mineral springs, and is identified by weeping.</p><p>In Candomblé Ketu she is the deity of fresh water and the patron of gestation, receiving the prayers of women who wish to have children and protecting small children until they begin to speak; in Candomblé Bantu she is Nkisi Ndandalunda, the Lady of Fertility and Moon. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santeria">Santería</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a>, brought over by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba people</a> during the transatlantic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, Ochún is syncretised with Our Lady of Charity and is distinct from Ozun, a masculine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orisha">orisha</a> associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oshun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
